00:01:10.679 --> 00:01:15.369
Google.org, Novartis,
Fastly, and General Motors.

00:01:15.369 --> 00:01:17.209
And then last but not least,

00:01:17.209 --> 00:01:19.789
I want to remind folks that
there is a podcast version

00:01:19.789 --> 00:01:23.389
of this live stream, "Homeroom
with Sal, The Podcast,"

00:01:23.389 --> 00:01:25.829
wherever you get your podcasts.

00:01:25.829 --> 00:01:29.509
So with that, I am super
excited to welcome back

00:01:29.509 --> 00:01:33.829
Vas Narasimhan back to our live stream.

00:01:33.829 --> 00:01:35.849
And, you know, in full disclosure,

00:01:35.849 --> 00:01:39.169
y'all are one of our
sponsors so thank you also.

00:01:39.169 --> 00:01:42.049
And people should also
know I'm somewhat biased

00:01:42.049 --> 00:01:46.489
towards you and Novartis (laughs)
so take everything we say

00:01:46.489 --> 00:01:47.509
with a grain of salt.

00:01:47.509 --> 00:01:48.642
But welcome back Vas.

00:01:49.719 --> 00:01:52.219
- Great to be here, Sal,
and I've even put on a,

00:01:52.219 --> 00:01:54.549
my kids gave me a Kahn Academy sticker

00:01:54.549 --> 00:01:56.439
to really be a part of the cause,

00:01:56.439 --> 00:01:58.599
so we're thrilled to support Kahn Academy.

00:01:58.599 --> 00:01:59.982
I think it's amazing what all of you do.

00:01:59.982 --> 00:02:02.659
I look forward to the discussions.

00:02:02.659 --> 00:02:04.289
- You know, I'm both
heartened and disappointed

00:02:04.289 --> 00:02:05.459
by what you just said.

00:02:05.459 --> 00:02:08.079
I'm heartened that you're
wearing a Kahn Academy sticker.

00:02:08.079 --> 00:02:10.559
I'm disappointed that
your kids had to force you

00:02:10.559 --> 00:02:11.392
to wear that.

00:02:11.392 --> 00:02:13.571
I'd like to think that in
your day-to-day management

00:02:13.571 --> 00:02:17.369
of running one of the largest
pharma companies in the world,

00:02:17.369 --> 00:02:19.699
you wear a Kahn Academy sticker
but maybe that will change.

00:02:19.699 --> 00:02:21.249
- I'll take that on advisement, Sal.

00:02:21.249 --> 00:02:22.599
I'll take it on advisement.

00:02:24.389 --> 00:02:27.099
- Well, you know, maybe
a good place to start

00:02:27.099 --> 00:02:29.547
and actually I have a
question, obviously we're in,

00:02:29.547 --> 00:02:31.931
I guess, I don't know what
we'd call it, year two now

00:02:31.931 --> 00:02:34.029
of COVID-19 some people are saying

00:02:34.029 --> 00:02:37.319
it should be called COVID 20
now with the Delta variant,

00:02:37.319 --> 00:02:39.322
and I've heard this word syndemic,

00:02:40.223 --> 00:02:43.229
and I'm hearing it actually
in, kind of pharma,

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:44.069
medical circles.

00:02:44.069 --> 00:02:45.719
My wife's a doctor as well.

00:02:45.719 --> 00:02:47.199
What is a syndemic?

00:02:48.995 --> 00:02:51.499
- A syndemic is the term we use kind of

00:02:51.499 --> 00:02:53.689
for a parallel epidemic that's happening

00:02:53.689 --> 00:02:54.522
alongside the pandemic.

00:02:54.522 --> 00:02:57.319
I mean, what we saw throughout last year,

00:02:57.319 --> 00:02:59.939
because unfortunately, as COVID took hold

00:02:59.939 --> 00:03:02.169
across healthcare systems,
if patients didn't get

00:03:02.169 --> 00:03:05.291
the routine care they need
for things like cancer,

00:03:05.291 --> 00:03:08.039
cardiovascular disease, lung disease,

00:03:08.039 --> 00:03:10.209
a range of different diseases.

00:03:10.209 --> 00:03:11.989
Part of that were
patients were afraid to go

00:03:11.989 --> 00:03:12.822
to the hospital.

00:03:12.822 --> 00:03:15.769
A big part of that were
hospitals were just so busy

00:03:15.769 --> 00:03:17.539
caring for COVID patients.

00:03:17.539 --> 00:03:19.779
And what that leads to is
when patients aren't getting

00:03:19.779 --> 00:03:23.369
their normal care for
noncommunicable diseases

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:26.449
like heart disease or cancer,
those diseases get worse.

00:03:26.449 --> 00:03:28.529
And actually it's even harder
to treat those patients

00:03:28.529 --> 00:03:29.609
later on.

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:32.759
Now we saw that improve in
the first part of this year,

00:03:32.759 --> 00:03:35.159
as it looked like things
were getting better.

00:03:35.159 --> 00:03:38.489
Unfortunately now, as Delta
variant takes hold again,

00:03:38.489 --> 00:03:41.399
we're seeing it again be an issue.

00:03:41.399 --> 00:03:43.999
I'm hopeful that healthcare
systems can figure out

00:03:43.999 --> 00:03:45.459
a way to continue to see patients,

00:03:45.459 --> 00:03:47.009
so they get the care they need.

00:03:48.269 --> 00:03:49.359
- Yeah, I mean, it's a
fascinating question,

00:03:49.359 --> 00:03:51.969
because we've talked a
lot about school closures

00:03:51.969 --> 00:03:53.549
and learning loss, and
it's one of these things

00:03:53.549 --> 00:03:56.129
where we as human beings,
we'll obviously watch the news

00:03:56.129 --> 00:03:58.879
and we'll see people who
are getting hospitalized

00:03:58.879 --> 00:04:01.649
and intubated or worse
because of the pandemic.

00:04:01.649 --> 00:04:02.919
So that feels very urgent.

00:04:02.919 --> 00:04:04.951
And then a lot of what we
try to do at Kahn Academy is

00:04:04.951 --> 00:04:07.399
remind folks, well,
there's also side effects

00:04:07.399 --> 00:04:08.809
of kids not being in school.

00:04:08.809 --> 00:04:11.429
In fact, long-term, there could
be even larger side effects,

00:04:11.429 --> 00:04:14.319
more harm to more kids than
even the pandemic itself.

00:04:14.319 --> 00:04:15.239
And what you're bringing up is

00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:17.719
even though our healthcare
system is operating

00:04:17.719 --> 00:04:21.029
and doing incredible things
when the pandemic picks up

00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:23.619
and especially once ICUs
fill up or people are afraid

00:04:23.619 --> 00:04:25.979
to go to the hospital,
it's creating a bit of

00:04:25.979 --> 00:04:28.892
a health loss, a health debt,

00:04:28.892 --> 00:04:30.999
which my wife has also described.

00:04:30.999 --> 00:04:33.089
Like she just started, now
that she's seeing more patients

00:04:33.089 --> 00:04:34.328
in person, she's seeing how much,

00:04:34.328 --> 00:04:36.428
how many of her patients
have really degraded

00:04:36.428 --> 00:04:40.349
in the time that they were afraid

00:04:40.349 --> 00:04:41.939
or they couldn't come to the hospital.

00:04:41.939 --> 00:04:43.849
How does Novartis,

00:04:43.849 --> 00:04:45.349
you know, before we started recording,

00:04:45.349 --> 00:04:47.289
I actually didn't fully appreciate,

00:04:47.289 --> 00:04:49.079
a lot of people when they
talk about the vaccines,

00:04:49.079 --> 00:04:51.619
they're talking about
Pfizer, Moderna, and J&amp;J,

00:04:51.619 --> 00:04:52.799
but I didn't realize
that y'all were actually

00:04:52.799 --> 00:04:55.849
one of the larger producers of vaccines,

00:04:55.849 --> 00:04:58.099
but above and beyond, I'm
curious how that's been going,

00:04:58.099 --> 00:05:01.339
but above and beyond that,
when you're running a large,

00:05:01.339 --> 00:05:03.359
one of the largest pharma
companies in the world,

00:05:03.359 --> 00:05:06.237
I believe the largest
producer of pharmaceuticals,

00:05:06.237 --> 00:05:10.228
you know, what is the
pandemic or the syndemic,

00:05:10.228 --> 00:05:12.199
has that changed what y'all are doing?

00:05:12.199 --> 00:05:15.139
And does it change how you
think about what your goal is

00:05:15.139 --> 00:05:16.152
as an organization?

00:05:17.799 --> 00:05:19.599
- It's had a lot of different impacts.

00:05:19.599 --> 00:05:22.389
Just to give your
listeners a sense of scope,

00:05:22.389 --> 00:05:25.209
I mean, so we are about 110,000 people,

00:05:25.209 --> 00:05:26.981
operate in 150 countries.

00:05:26.981 --> 00:05:30.526
We produce about 70 billion
doses of medicine a year,

00:05:30.526 --> 00:05:33.965
and in a usual year, we reach
about 800 million patients

00:05:33.965 --> 00:05:35.999
with those medicines.

00:05:35.999 --> 00:05:39.629
So a very, very large, large scale.

00:05:39.629 --> 00:05:42.179
Now, of course, when you have
something like this disrupt

00:05:42.179 --> 00:05:44.959
healthcare systems it
impacts our business,

00:05:44.959 --> 00:05:47.929
also impacts our ability
to run clinical trials.

00:05:47.929 --> 00:05:50.166
Many of our clinical
trials were disrupted.

00:05:50.166 --> 00:05:52.149
We've had to do a lot of
work to keep clinical trials

00:05:52.149 --> 00:05:56.299
and clinical research going
at an appropriate pace.

00:05:56.299 --> 00:05:58.529
One of the other challenges that we see

00:05:58.529 --> 00:06:00.189
from a clinical standpoint is,

00:06:00.189 --> 00:06:01.649
I'm sure your wife is seeing this as well.

00:06:01.649 --> 00:06:04.529
If you see cancer patients
who are treated later,

00:06:04.529 --> 00:06:07.239
usually the medicines have
a harder and harder time

00:06:07.239 --> 00:06:09.679
having their effect if
patients are more progressed,

00:06:09.679 --> 00:06:13.050
similarly in heart
disease or lung disease.

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:16.069
So we're trying to educate policy makers.

00:06:16.069 --> 00:06:18.639
I think health ministers
that I speak with are

00:06:18.639 --> 00:06:20.418
very aware of this challenge,

00:06:20.418 --> 00:06:22.289
but it is a very human challenge.

00:06:22.289 --> 00:06:24.519
In the end, people can
get very afraid to go

00:06:24.519 --> 00:06:27.249
to the hospital and they
put off routine care

00:06:27.249 --> 00:06:30.369
or very routine screening
in the case of many

00:06:30.369 --> 00:06:32.059
of these diseases.

00:06:32.059 --> 00:06:33.749
And you also asked about
vaccine production.

00:06:33.749 --> 00:06:36.419
I mean, our part I think is small

00:06:36.419 --> 00:06:38.529
relative to many of the companies

00:06:38.529 --> 00:06:40.159
that are the innovators of these vaccines,

00:06:40.159 --> 00:06:42.569
but given our very large
manufacturing footprint,

00:06:42.569 --> 00:06:45.199
we are able to produce
vaccines here in Switzerland

00:06:45.199 --> 00:06:47.399
and our facilities in Austria,

00:06:47.399 --> 00:06:50.019
to support, you know, the global pandemic.

00:06:50.019 --> 00:06:52.129
And it's something that
I think we feel like is

00:06:52.129 --> 00:06:55.842
part of our responsibility as
a large healthcare company.

00:06:57.043 --> 00:06:58.239
- And I never thought about
it, I mean we were talking

00:06:58.239 --> 00:07:01.059
about the syndemic just now
and how other types of things

00:07:01.059 --> 00:07:03.209
could be slowed down, or
there could be a health debt,

00:07:03.209 --> 00:07:04.409
but I never thought about the impact

00:07:04.409 --> 00:07:06.639
on clinical trials themselves.

00:07:06.639 --> 00:07:09.489
Do you think industry-wide
this is actually slowing down

00:07:09.489 --> 00:07:12.642
some drugs that could have
gone to market sooner?

00:07:12.642 --> 00:07:15.092
- I mean, I think it remains to be seen.

00:07:15.092 --> 00:07:16.919
I mean, companies have
been working really hard.

00:07:16.919 --> 00:07:19.489
Like for instance, one of the
big transitions we've seen is

00:07:19.489 --> 00:07:22.439
just to use technology to keep
people on clinical trials.

00:07:22.439 --> 00:07:26.649
So clinical trials used to be
a very human-heavy endeavor,

00:07:26.649 --> 00:07:29.159
and now we're using remote monitoring

00:07:29.159 --> 00:07:31.049
to monitor the patients
on our clinical trials,

00:07:31.049 --> 00:07:32.312
keep them on study.

00:07:32.312 --> 00:07:34.939
But I do think you'll see
some impacts for sure.

00:07:34.939 --> 00:07:36.599
I mean, if you're just
thinking about the disruption,

00:07:36.599 --> 00:07:39.417
a clinical trial anyway is
such a hard thing to run.

00:07:39.417 --> 00:07:41.919
If suddenly like an entire country says,

00:07:41.919 --> 00:07:43.557
no patients can come into the hospital

00:07:43.557 --> 00:07:46.399
and then suddenly that
country is shut down

00:07:46.399 --> 00:07:47.349
for your clinical trial.

00:07:47.349 --> 00:07:49.129
Then you have to go to another country.

00:07:49.129 --> 00:07:51.299
These create disruptions in your datasets.

00:07:51.299 --> 00:07:52.699
And then when you run the statistics,

00:07:52.699 --> 00:07:54.429
statistics that many people learn about

00:07:54.429 --> 00:07:56.937
through Khan Academy,
those statistics get harder

00:07:56.937 --> 00:07:59.919
to run because you have to
account for all of these

00:07:59.919 --> 00:08:02.625
disruptions that have
happened in the studies.

00:08:02.625 --> 00:08:04.679
I would say though overall,

00:08:04.679 --> 00:08:08.479
at least we've been able
to manage reasonably well.

00:08:08.479 --> 00:08:09.479
- Yeah, no, no.

00:08:09.479 --> 00:08:10.519
It's fascinating.

00:08:10.519 --> 00:08:12.489
And I will, as an aside,
give you full credit.

00:08:12.489 --> 00:08:13.489
You are the best guest

00:08:13.489 --> 00:08:15.289
when it comes to plugging Khan Academy.

00:08:15.289 --> 00:08:17.229
Beyond the sticker on your shirt,

00:08:17.229 --> 00:08:19.419
if you mention statistics,
you remind everyone

00:08:19.419 --> 00:08:20.899
it can be learned on Kahn Academy.

00:08:20.899 --> 00:08:22.686
So... (laughing)

00:08:22.686 --> 00:08:26.489
- You know, my son just
completed the statistics course

00:08:26.489 --> 00:08:27.349
on Kahn Academy.

00:08:27.349 --> 00:08:29.029
So that's why I knew about the statistics.

00:08:29.029 --> 00:08:32.119
I was getting my own refresher
on some of these topics.

00:08:32.119 --> 00:08:34.289
- I think I'm going to send
you another sticker, Vas.

00:08:34.289 --> 00:08:37.331
You deserve it.
(Vas laughs)

00:08:37.331 --> 00:08:38.435
- Well, I mean,

00:08:38.435 --> 00:08:40.571
getting to more serious topics,

00:08:40.571 --> 00:08:44.259
which I unfortunately
took us on a tangent,

00:08:44.259 --> 00:08:47.869
the vaccine itself, you know, thank God.

00:08:47.869 --> 00:08:50.879
Thank the universe that we now have them.

00:08:50.879 --> 00:08:52.918
Obviously it's somewhat been politicized

00:08:52.918 --> 00:08:55.995
where not everyone is taking them,

00:08:55.995 --> 00:08:58.936
but what's your view of how
this is going to play out?

00:08:58.936 --> 00:09:01.739
You know, I remember
last year I was watching

00:09:01.739 --> 00:09:03.999
some news channel and there
was some epidemiologist

00:09:03.999 --> 00:09:05.859
who seemed like doom and gloom.

00:09:05.859 --> 00:09:08.349
Who's like, "This thing's
always going to be here.

00:09:08.349 --> 00:09:09.329
It's gonna be like the flu.

00:09:09.329 --> 00:09:11.139
It's gonna be constantly evolving.

00:09:11.139 --> 00:09:14.359
We're just gonna have to live
with COVID in the future."

00:09:14.359 --> 00:09:16.469
And I remember at the
time, everyone, I think,

00:09:16.469 --> 00:09:18.379
thought that guy was a wet blanket.

00:09:18.379 --> 00:09:20.339
Like it's not going to be that way.

00:09:20.339 --> 00:09:23.149
Now it's starting to feel like
that guy might've been right.

00:09:23.149 --> 00:09:24.559
Obviously you have the Delta variant,

00:09:24.559 --> 00:09:26.209
people are talking about booster shots.

00:09:26.209 --> 00:09:30.499
People are talking about, do
we need a Delta aware vaccine?

00:09:30.499 --> 00:09:31.826
What's your view of all of this?

00:09:31.826 --> 00:09:35.349
Are people working on
a Delta aware vaccine?

00:09:35.349 --> 00:09:36.999
Is this going to constantly evolve?

00:09:36.999 --> 00:09:39.349
Will there be a, you know,
a gamma, and an omega,

00:09:39.349 --> 00:09:40.379
a COVID 21?

00:09:40.379 --> 00:09:42.759
How do you think this is going to happen?

00:09:42.759 --> 00:09:45.129
- Yeah, so a few big principle points

00:09:45.129 --> 00:09:47.989
I think it's worth for the
listeners to understand.

00:09:47.989 --> 00:09:49.709
I mean, one we're surrounded by viruses.

00:09:49.709 --> 00:09:51.299
I mean, if you think of a cup of seawater,

00:09:51.299 --> 00:09:53.659
you have billions or trillions of viruses.

00:09:53.659 --> 00:09:56.499
And viruses actually probably enabled

00:09:56.499 --> 00:09:59.079
the first prokaryotic
and eukaryotic cells,

00:09:59.079 --> 00:10:00.849
maybe even multicellular life.

00:10:00.849 --> 00:10:03.299
So we live with viruses.

00:10:03.299 --> 00:10:05.129
And then when you look at Corona viruses,

00:10:05.129 --> 00:10:07.929
there are four normally
circulating Corona viruses

00:10:07.929 --> 00:10:09.449
that cause the common cold,

00:10:09.449 --> 00:10:11.848
alongside many other
viruses that we deal with

00:10:11.848 --> 00:10:16.129
as the human species and
that we've adapted to.

00:10:16.129 --> 00:10:19.129
So usually with these types
of respiratory viruses,

00:10:19.129 --> 00:10:21.429
the expectation is
eventually we will reach

00:10:21.429 --> 00:10:24.659
an equilibrium state
where the virus evolves

00:10:24.659 --> 00:10:26.885
to a place where it is causing

00:10:26.885 --> 00:10:30.499
minimally symptomatic
disease, but enabling spread.

00:10:30.499 --> 00:10:33.489
Think about the common cold,
runny nose, all those things.

00:10:33.489 --> 00:10:36.109
And our immune systems get primed enough

00:10:36.109 --> 00:10:38.873
that we can handle that
assault, because we have memory

00:10:38.873 --> 00:10:42.729
in our T-cells, we also
have memory in our B cells

00:10:42.729 --> 00:10:45.059
and we're able to deal with like, as we do

00:10:45.059 --> 00:10:46.889
with other Corona viruses.

00:10:46.889 --> 00:10:50.259
What we're observing right
now is that process happening.

00:10:50.259 --> 00:10:52.299
And the virus is mutating.

00:10:52.299 --> 00:10:53.929
Our immune systems are developing.

00:10:53.929 --> 00:10:57.089
Vaccines are hopefully
enabling our immune systems

00:10:57.089 --> 00:10:58.039
to get stronger.

00:10:58.039 --> 00:11:00.949
They clearly are, and then
are enabling us to adapt.

00:11:00.949 --> 00:11:04.329
And the question is how
can we as a global society

00:11:04.329 --> 00:11:06.749
best manage that to minimize the impact

00:11:06.749 --> 00:11:09.049
as we get to that equilibrium?

00:11:09.049 --> 00:11:12.289
So that's kind of the way I
think about the big picture.

00:11:12.289 --> 00:11:15.829
It's hard to know when exactly
that equilibrium will happen,

00:11:15.829 --> 00:11:16.662
but it will.

00:11:16.662 --> 00:11:18.219
But I think it's reasonable to expect

00:11:18.219 --> 00:11:20.439
that this Corona virus will become

00:11:20.439 --> 00:11:22.709
a normal circulating Corona virus.

00:11:22.709 --> 00:11:25.109
like we have other Corona viruses

00:11:25.109 --> 00:11:28.409
and cause symptomatic respiratory illness,

00:11:28.409 --> 00:11:31.379
but probably nothing severe
because we have adapted to it

00:11:31.379 --> 00:11:32.859
as a species.

00:11:32.859 --> 00:11:34.659
Now, in the meantime, the question is

00:11:34.659 --> 00:11:38.039
how much do we need to keep
working on future vaccines?

00:11:38.039 --> 00:11:41.099
And I think it's an open question.

00:11:41.099 --> 00:11:43.889
I think we have to be driven by data.

00:11:43.889 --> 00:11:45.849
The data would seem to suggest

00:11:45.849 --> 00:11:47.479
that the current vaccines produce

00:11:47.479 --> 00:11:50.149
a very robust memory response.

00:11:50.149 --> 00:11:52.509
It also seems naturally
infected people are producing

00:11:52.509 --> 00:11:54.030
a memory response.

00:11:54.030 --> 00:11:56.749
That's why we see less severe disease

00:11:56.749 --> 00:11:58.539
and less hospitalizations and deaths

00:11:58.539 --> 00:12:01.119
in people who are previously vaccinated,

00:12:01.119 --> 00:12:03.419
but people who are previously
vaccinated are able

00:12:03.419 --> 00:12:05.699
to get infected and spread.

00:12:05.699 --> 00:12:07.829
And so we're gonna have to see,

00:12:07.829 --> 00:12:11.239
I think in the coming months,
how the data plays out,

00:12:11.239 --> 00:12:15.189
how the booster shots really
look from a data standpoint.

00:12:15.189 --> 00:12:17.729
And I think the health
authorities then will figure out

00:12:17.729 --> 00:12:19.669
what the right right path forward is.

00:12:19.669 --> 00:12:21.349
But I think it's worth remembering

00:12:21.349 --> 00:12:23.639
that this is going to be an equilibrium

00:12:23.639 --> 00:12:25.099
that happens over time.

00:12:25.099 --> 00:12:27.709
Also, I think it's an
incredible accomplishment

00:12:27.709 --> 00:12:29.869
of modern science that
by the end of this year,

00:12:29.869 --> 00:12:33.079
my guess is, we'll have
over 10 billion doses

00:12:33.079 --> 00:12:35.189
of vaccine produced.

00:12:35.189 --> 00:12:38.629
Which is really less than
18, 20 months from when

00:12:38.629 --> 00:12:39.936
we first characterized this virus.

00:12:39.936 --> 00:12:42.312
That's an astonishing
scientific achievement.

00:12:43.609 --> 00:12:44.999
- No, it is incredible.

00:12:44.999 --> 00:12:46.147
And you know,

00:12:46.147 --> 00:12:48.139
just following on this notion

00:12:48.139 --> 00:12:49.739
that vaccines are constantly evolving.

00:12:49.739 --> 00:12:52.424
I mean, actually, I redid a
Kahn Academy video yesterday

00:12:52.424 --> 00:12:55.076
about evolution and natural selection.

00:12:55.076 --> 00:12:56.736
And I know sometimes people say,

00:12:56.736 --> 00:12:57.569
"Oh, well this goes over,"

00:12:57.569 --> 00:12:59.309
Or you know, "Something that
you don't necessarily see,

00:12:59.309 --> 00:13:00.639
it occurs over millions of years."

00:13:00.639 --> 00:13:02.429
I'm like, "No, you're
seeing it before your eyes"

00:13:02.429 --> 00:13:03.949
with these variants of these viruses.

00:13:03.949 --> 00:13:05.759
And you're seeing it every
year with the flu virus.

00:13:05.759 --> 00:13:07.269
Evolution is happening.

00:13:07.269 --> 00:13:11.299
And to your point, selection
pressure for a virus,

00:13:11.299 --> 00:13:14.329
it wouldn't naturally select for viruses

00:13:14.329 --> 00:13:15.589
that are necessarily more deadly.

00:13:15.589 --> 00:13:19.289
It's actually not in the virus's
interest to kill the host.

00:13:19.289 --> 00:13:21.639
Now I think everyone's
nightmare scenarios are

00:13:21.639 --> 00:13:24.079
something that spreads like Delta,

00:13:24.079 --> 00:13:25.989
you know, it's very easy to spread,

00:13:25.989 --> 00:13:27.909
it can kind of evade at
least the first phases

00:13:27.909 --> 00:13:29.649
of the immune system,

00:13:29.649 --> 00:13:31.709
and then kind of stays
dormant while it spreads

00:13:31.709 --> 00:13:35.012
and then becomes worse
and then becomes deadly.

00:13:35.012 --> 00:13:38.959
Is it just kind of dark
science fiction thinking

00:13:38.959 --> 00:13:40.739
that we could go in that direction?

00:13:40.739 --> 00:13:42.559
Because, I guess the argument is

00:13:42.559 --> 00:13:45.549
evolution wouldn't select
for something like that.

00:13:45.549 --> 00:13:47.203
It could randomly happen because we have

00:13:47.203 --> 00:13:50.209
so many hosts right now, billions of hosts

00:13:50.209 --> 00:13:54.259
each with billions of viruses in them,

00:13:54.259 --> 00:13:57.302
that is maybe accelerating
the evolution to some degree.

00:13:58.789 --> 00:14:01.579
- Yeah, I think it's, I
mean, certainly all possible,

00:14:01.579 --> 00:14:03.919
but in general, what we do observe,

00:14:03.919 --> 00:14:06.889
and viruses, again, if you
look at parainfluenza viruses

00:14:06.889 --> 00:14:09.939
and rhinoviruses and other Corona viruses,

00:14:09.939 --> 00:14:13.139
the tropism and the way
they tend to evolve is

00:14:13.139 --> 00:14:15.869
to be minimally symptomatic,

00:14:15.869 --> 00:14:18.549
and very transmissible
because they actually want you

00:14:18.549 --> 00:14:20.869
to move around and behave normally,

00:14:20.869 --> 00:14:22.489
because that's how the gene,

00:14:22.489 --> 00:14:25.654
as you likely taught in the
natural selection class,

00:14:25.654 --> 00:14:28.259
will then pass on to the next generation

00:14:28.259 --> 00:14:30.559
and the viruses will propagate.

00:14:30.559 --> 00:14:32.770
If you look at other examples, things like

00:14:32.770 --> 00:14:36.752
some of the hemorrhagic fevers
that come out of Africa,

00:14:37.829 --> 00:14:39.502
whether it's Ebola, Marburg, Lassa,

00:14:39.502 --> 00:14:41.619
they have very high case fatality rates,

00:14:41.619 --> 00:14:42.989
but they usually burn out,

00:14:42.989 --> 00:14:45.690
because eventually they
kill the host so fast

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:47.559
that transmission doesn't happen

00:14:47.559 --> 00:14:49.859
and those tend to be very contained.

00:14:49.859 --> 00:14:52.489
So that is what the history would tell us

00:14:52.489 --> 00:14:55.809
and how you would expect
for this to play out.

00:14:55.809 --> 00:14:57.616
I think even if you had a very,

00:14:57.616 --> 00:15:00.749
you know, more virulent strain, again,

00:15:00.749 --> 00:15:04.039
the human immune system is designed to,

00:15:04.039 --> 00:15:06.149
especially for these kinds
of respiratory viruses,

00:15:06.149 --> 00:15:09.119
to develop a very robust immune response.

00:15:09.119 --> 00:15:11.509
But what you invariably have
when you have that happen,

00:15:11.509 --> 00:15:13.959
is you will have a bump in severe cases

00:15:13.959 --> 00:15:17.089
when such a virus comes about.

00:15:17.089 --> 00:15:19.659
But I overall, Sal, remain optimistic.

00:15:19.659 --> 00:15:22.829
I mean, when I look at these
kinds of respiratory viruses

00:15:22.829 --> 00:15:25.909
and how in a big picture view,
how they tend to settle in,

00:15:25.909 --> 00:15:27.729
in human populations,

00:15:27.729 --> 00:15:30.879
they tend to trend towards
an equilibrium state

00:15:30.879 --> 00:15:34.289
of minimally symptomatic
or mildly symptomatic,

00:15:34.289 --> 00:15:37.119
cold-like respiratory condition.

00:15:37.119 --> 00:15:38.739
Then we wouldn't even notice.

00:15:38.739 --> 00:15:39.986
And we would just say,

00:15:39.986 --> 00:15:43.019
"Oh, it's just another
circulating cold virus."

00:15:43.019 --> 00:15:45.379
- Right, and we have
historical examples of that.

00:15:45.379 --> 00:15:46.709
The flu, obviously at one point,

00:15:46.709 --> 00:15:49.019
the flu was scary, very scary.

00:15:49.019 --> 00:15:50.759
It's still not completely not scary,

00:15:50.759 --> 00:15:53.269
but it's much more maintainable.

00:15:53.269 --> 00:15:55.289
A big question, and it connects with

00:15:55.289 --> 00:15:57.409
what we often talk about, education,

00:15:57.409 --> 00:16:00.389
is vaccines for younger people.

00:16:00.389 --> 00:16:05.319
What's your view of
why it is taking longer

00:16:05.319 --> 00:16:09.249
and what we might expect to
see in the next few months

00:16:09.249 --> 00:16:11.669
when we might be able to
vaccinate 12 and under?

00:16:11.669 --> 00:16:13.199
And I am also curious, I've had a very

00:16:13.199 --> 00:16:15.229
US specific view on that.

00:16:15.229 --> 00:16:16.229
What are other countries doing

00:16:16.229 --> 00:16:18.152
on the vaccination of younger people?

00:16:20.139 --> 00:16:21.429
- So, first I think it's, again,

00:16:21.429 --> 00:16:24.529
worth looking at how
astonishing the achievement

00:16:24.529 --> 00:16:26.609
of pediatric vaccination has been.

00:16:26.609 --> 00:16:29.509
I mean, if you look, actually,
back over the last century,

00:16:29.509 --> 00:16:31.639
the biggest public health intervention,

00:16:31.639 --> 00:16:34.599
along with clean water, that's
happened in human history is

00:16:34.599 --> 00:16:36.189
actually pediatric vaccination.

00:16:36.189 --> 00:16:37.649
I mean, it's astonishing

00:16:37.649 --> 00:16:40.589
what pediatric vaccination
has been able to accomplish.

00:16:40.589 --> 00:16:44.029
And we often forget that
because we just don't see mumps

00:16:44.029 --> 00:16:47.039
and measles and rubella
and all of these conditions

00:16:47.039 --> 00:16:47.899
any longer.

00:16:47.899 --> 00:16:51.049
I mean, even pneumococcal pneumonia,

00:16:51.049 --> 00:16:52.909
and meningococcal meningitis,

00:16:52.909 --> 00:16:56.436
because these vaccines
have been so effective.

00:16:56.436 --> 00:16:58.889
And so one, I think it's
worth reminding ourselves

00:16:58.889 --> 00:17:01.169
that when we do get a
pediatric vaccine approved,

00:17:01.169 --> 00:17:04.029
people should really look
to vaccinate children.

00:17:04.029 --> 00:17:06.829
It's one of the most successful
public health interventions

00:17:06.829 --> 00:17:08.819
that we've ever had.

00:17:08.819 --> 00:17:11.148
Now, in terms of usually
how vaccines are developed

00:17:11.148 --> 00:17:14.399
and including drugs, as
well, is we step down

00:17:14.399 --> 00:17:17.529
through the age groups,
because we want to ensure

00:17:17.529 --> 00:17:19.429
that there's a good safety profile.

00:17:19.429 --> 00:17:22.099
Because children's immune systems,

00:17:22.099 --> 00:17:23.309
you, of course, want to ensure

00:17:23.309 --> 00:17:26.519
that you're not gonna have
any adverse consequences.

00:17:26.519 --> 00:17:28.989
And so I think that's what's
being systematically done.

00:17:28.989 --> 00:17:30.809
Usually, historically, what you do is

00:17:30.809 --> 00:17:33.246
you get down to 11 year
olds, then 2 to 10 year olds,

00:17:33.246 --> 00:17:35.579
and then under two years old.

00:17:35.579 --> 00:17:37.799
And again, it's worth
remembering that most children

00:17:37.799 --> 00:17:39.709
in the world get a lot of vaccines

00:17:39.709 --> 00:17:40.979
when they're two months of age.

00:17:40.979 --> 00:17:43.179
That's actually when we start vaccinating.

00:17:43.179 --> 00:17:46.459
So we'll see how the data
develops on that front.

00:17:46.459 --> 00:17:48.449
But my guess is over time,
we'll be getting down

00:17:48.449 --> 00:17:50.469
in those younger and younger age groups.

00:17:50.469 --> 00:17:52.739
I think many countries
around the world are similar

00:17:52.739 --> 00:17:56.529
to the US now, at the 12 plus age range.

00:17:56.529 --> 00:17:59.219
And then I think over the coming months,

00:17:59.219 --> 00:18:01.599
we'll be able to get into
the pediatric age range

00:18:01.599 --> 00:18:02.932
and then younger as well.

00:18:04.329 --> 00:18:06.519
- And does it change depending on what,

00:18:06.519 --> 00:18:08.709
I'm assuming there's
certain safety profiles,

00:18:08.709 --> 00:18:12.169
you have to test the vaccine
in a certain scale normally,

00:18:12.169 --> 00:18:14.149
but when you're in something
of a public health emergency

00:18:14.149 --> 00:18:16.339
like this, do they change the rules

00:18:16.339 --> 00:18:18.519
or do they just try to
run the trials faster?

00:18:18.519 --> 00:18:20.152
How do they expedite things?

00:18:21.129 --> 00:18:22.829
- Well first, I would say this vaccine,

00:18:22.829 --> 00:18:25.684
these vaccines have the
biggest safety databases

00:18:25.684 --> 00:18:27.082
of any vaccines, right now.

00:18:27.082 --> 00:18:30.880
We have billions of doses, and
so the denominators here are

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:34.619
remarkable to look at any adverse event.

00:18:34.619 --> 00:18:37.259
And while I think that
understandably there is

00:18:37.259 --> 00:18:40.159
news media pick up whenever
there is any sort of event,

00:18:40.159 --> 00:18:42.219
it's important to recognize
that in most cases,

00:18:42.219 --> 00:18:45.161
these are astonishingly
rare and you have more risk

00:18:45.161 --> 00:18:47.769
getting in your car in
the morning than you do

00:18:47.769 --> 00:18:51.949
of any of these very rare
adverse events for vaccines.

00:18:51.949 --> 00:18:54.929
And it's very hard as you
know, for the human mind

00:18:54.929 --> 00:18:57.299
to assess risks, but I think
one of the useful things

00:18:57.299 --> 00:18:59.179
to do is compare it to
the other risks you take

00:18:59.179 --> 00:19:01.159
every single day, living your life,

00:19:01.159 --> 00:19:04.325
and you see that these risks
are actually quite small.

00:19:04.325 --> 00:19:06.749
The way the clinical
trials are, they're using

00:19:06.749 --> 00:19:08.379
pretty standard clinical trials.

00:19:08.379 --> 00:19:10.329
And I would say my own
experience in vaccines,

00:19:10.329 --> 00:19:12.619
these numbers are very
large that they're using.

00:19:12.619 --> 00:19:14.699
I mean, most of the databases here

00:19:14.699 --> 00:19:19.429
in the clinical studies are
50,000 to 100,000 plus patients.

00:19:19.429 --> 00:19:21.509
Those are very robust databases

00:19:21.509 --> 00:19:23.499
to identify significant safety signals,

00:19:23.499 --> 00:19:24.539
including in children.

00:19:24.539 --> 00:19:27.414
So I would feel very
confident that the regulators

00:19:27.414 --> 00:19:29.864
and the companies are
doing the right thing here.

00:19:30.869 --> 00:19:31.702
- Yeah.

00:19:31.702 --> 00:19:34.879
And, on that last point about
stack ranking the things

00:19:34.879 --> 00:19:37.649
that are dangerous in life,
until we have a vaccine

00:19:37.649 --> 00:19:39.499
for younger kids, at least here in the US,

00:19:39.499 --> 00:19:41.418
we've heard stories now
that school is starting up,

00:19:41.418 --> 00:19:44.859
you know, pretty scary stories of 10, 20%

00:19:44.859 --> 00:19:46.319
of kindergarteners not showing up,

00:19:46.319 --> 00:19:48.859
because parents are afraid
of the Delta variant.

00:19:48.859 --> 00:19:49.739
And it is scary.

00:19:49.739 --> 00:19:52.459
I have one of my oldest
got vaccinated, he's 12,

00:19:52.459 --> 00:19:54.439
but my other two are not vaccinated.

00:19:54.439 --> 00:19:57.519
So I definitely feel that,
but at the same time,

00:19:57.519 --> 00:19:59.827
just my own curiosity,
I started looking up

00:19:59.827 --> 00:20:01.879
hospitalizations and deaths,

00:20:01.879 --> 00:20:04.109
and it does not, even the
Delta variant does not seem

00:20:04.109 --> 00:20:05.029
dramatically different.

00:20:05.029 --> 00:20:07.939
I might be wrong or maybe the
data hasn't fully come in yet,

00:20:07.939 --> 00:20:09.415
with the flu.

00:20:09.415 --> 00:20:12.279
And both of them are actually less likely

00:20:12.279 --> 00:20:13.651
to kill a young person to your point

00:20:13.651 --> 00:20:16.088
than a car or a swimming pool.

00:20:16.088 --> 00:20:19.399
So, you know, for those of you in the...

00:20:19.399 --> 00:20:22.319
what would be your advice to a parent

00:20:22.319 --> 00:20:24.069
who's scared right now,

00:20:24.069 --> 00:20:26.472
especially if they can't
get their kids vaccinated?

00:20:27.539 --> 00:20:30.549
- Yeah, I think, again,
when you look at the data,

00:20:30.549 --> 00:20:33.219
it would suggest that
especially in younger children,

00:20:33.219 --> 00:20:35.709
that the risk here is very small.

00:20:35.709 --> 00:20:37.799
I mean, even infected children,

00:20:37.799 --> 00:20:40.377
despite the one-off news media
reports that you're hearing,

00:20:40.377 --> 00:20:43.245
you know, you're seeing very
low rates of hospitalization

00:20:43.245 --> 00:20:45.759
or severe disease.

00:20:45.759 --> 00:20:48.069
And my guess is it's probably even better

00:20:48.069 --> 00:20:50.879
than we see in the data
because the denominator

00:20:50.879 --> 00:20:52.799
of number of children or adults

00:20:52.799 --> 00:20:54.379
who are asymptomatically infected

00:20:54.379 --> 00:20:56.139
or not captured is quite significant.

00:20:56.139 --> 00:20:57.989
And there've been very good models

00:20:57.989 --> 00:21:00.619
that have been produced
by different economists

00:21:00.619 --> 00:21:04.279
and epidemiologists that
would suggest 5, 10,

00:21:04.279 --> 00:21:08.662
maybe more fold under-counting
of the denominator,

00:21:08.662 --> 00:21:12.999
which means that the actual
rate of hospitalization,

00:21:12.999 --> 00:21:15.308
rate of severe disease,
rate of death is even lower

00:21:15.308 --> 00:21:18.899
than what we might think otherwise.

00:21:18.899 --> 00:21:21.020
So, you know, I would,

00:21:21.020 --> 00:21:23.549
if the school is open and the school has

00:21:23.549 --> 00:21:25.749
appropriate precautions,
I think the damage

00:21:25.749 --> 00:21:28.619
we could be doing to
children by not ensuring

00:21:28.619 --> 00:21:32.429
that they're continuing to
have their social development,

00:21:32.429 --> 00:21:34.099
their educational development,

00:21:34.099 --> 00:21:35.579
that's something we've
got to factor in too.

00:21:35.579 --> 00:21:36.819
I mean, one of the things that I think,

00:21:36.819 --> 00:21:39.829
I'm sure you're also
concerned about globally,

00:21:39.829 --> 00:21:42.269
the loss of years of education

00:21:42.269 --> 00:21:45.640
and the impact that will
have on the longterm mental

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:48.739
and social wellbeing for these generations

00:21:48.739 --> 00:21:50.829
of children is just substantial,

00:21:50.829 --> 00:21:52.299
and should be counted, right?

00:21:52.299 --> 00:21:55.519
And counted and considered
whenever we think about

00:21:55.519 --> 00:21:57.992
school lockdowns and school closures.

00:21:58.929 --> 00:22:00.849
- Yeah, couldn't agree more, but it is,

00:22:00.849 --> 00:22:02.799
it's gonna be interesting
how this plays out.

00:22:02.799 --> 00:22:05.079
Well, thank you for
indulging me and all of us.

00:22:05.079 --> 00:22:08.029
And, you know, I actually give
credit to Raina Rustenburg.

00:22:08.029 --> 00:22:09.089
They asked a couple of questions

00:22:09.089 --> 00:22:10.429
that I paraphrased a little bit.

00:22:10.429 --> 00:22:12.679
So I want to make sure that
that YouTube viewer gets

00:22:12.679 --> 00:22:14.329
proper credit for the questions.

00:22:14.329 --> 00:22:16.259
But I do want to shift a little bit.

00:22:16.259 --> 00:22:18.536
You know, I could talk for
hours about the vaccine

00:22:18.536 --> 00:22:20.459
and how all of this might evolve,

00:22:20.459 --> 00:22:23.569
but I also want to be
forward-looking post pandemic,

00:22:23.569 --> 00:22:24.939
or maybe it's related to the pandemic.

00:22:24.939 --> 00:22:27.209
I mean, you're running one of the largest

00:22:27.209 --> 00:22:29.222
pharmaceutical companies in the world.

00:22:29.222 --> 00:22:31.629
What are you excited about?

00:22:31.629 --> 00:22:33.374
Where is medicine going?

00:22:33.374 --> 00:22:36.869
I think medicine, a lot of
people compare education

00:22:36.869 --> 00:22:37.702
to medicine and they're like,

00:22:37.702 --> 00:22:39.659
"Look what medicine has
done in the last 50 years"

00:22:39.659 --> 00:22:41.779
and education, there's a
lot of good in education,

00:22:41.779 --> 00:22:43.529
but it looks like it's fairly similar

00:22:43.529 --> 00:22:44.709
to the way it was 50 years ago

00:22:44.709 --> 00:22:45.699
or a hundred years ago.

00:22:45.699 --> 00:22:47.619
Hopefully some of that
will change for the better,

00:22:47.619 --> 00:22:49.909
but what do you think
medicine is going to look like

00:22:49.909 --> 00:22:52.259
if we fast forward 5
years, 10 years, 20 years?

00:22:53.233 --> 00:22:54.229
- Yeah, it's interesting.

00:22:54.229 --> 00:22:56.019
I mean, right now we're undergoing,

00:22:56.019 --> 00:22:58.429
really a substantial
Renaissance in how we think

00:22:58.429 --> 00:23:01.054
about therapeutics in medicine.

00:23:01.054 --> 00:23:03.619
I mean, go again back in history,

00:23:03.619 --> 00:23:05.349
it was really in the early 1900s

00:23:05.349 --> 00:23:07.319
we began to think about medicine

00:23:07.319 --> 00:23:10.029
as being able to impact the human body.

00:23:10.029 --> 00:23:13.439
Before then we had much
less sophisticated views

00:23:13.439 --> 00:23:14.449
on how to impact health.

00:23:14.449 --> 00:23:17.109
We started with chemicals, so chemistry,

00:23:17.109 --> 00:23:20.079
small molecule chemicals
that would interact

00:23:20.079 --> 00:23:23.029
with proteins to try to
get a therapeutic effect.

00:23:23.029 --> 00:23:26.919
Around the 1970s, 1980s,
we moved into the world

00:23:26.919 --> 00:23:28.569
of producing large molecules,

00:23:28.569 --> 00:23:31.089
so proteins to impact proteins

00:23:31.089 --> 00:23:32.899
or proteins to bind other proteins

00:23:32.899 --> 00:23:35.049
or proteins to bind
other things in the body.

00:23:35.049 --> 00:23:36.279
That's called biologics.

00:23:36.279 --> 00:23:38.909
We had small molecules and biologics.

00:23:38.909 --> 00:23:40.879
And then there wasn't another revolution

00:23:40.879 --> 00:23:43.309
until pretty recently,
where now there's been

00:23:43.309 --> 00:23:45.540
an explosion of a few
different technologies.

00:23:45.540 --> 00:23:47.159
Some of them, we work on.

00:23:47.159 --> 00:23:49.919
Gene therapies, which allow us to edit

00:23:49.919 --> 00:23:52.929
the genome of patients to really correct

00:23:52.929 --> 00:23:55.249
for inherited abnormalities.

00:23:55.249 --> 00:23:57.939
You have cell therapies
which are taking cells

00:23:57.939 --> 00:23:59.569
out of the body, reprogramming them,

00:23:59.569 --> 00:24:01.837
using technologies like
you've heard of, like CRISPR

00:24:01.837 --> 00:24:04.824
that then reprogram the cells,
put them back in the body.

00:24:04.824 --> 00:24:08.689
We have RNA and maybe
everybody knows about mRNA,

00:24:08.689 --> 00:24:10.569
the other revolution that's happened

00:24:10.569 --> 00:24:12.169
that was years in the making,

00:24:12.169 --> 00:24:15.309
going back to work done
in the 1970s and 1980s,

00:24:15.309 --> 00:24:19.369
is to understand how
RNA modulates other RNA,

00:24:19.369 --> 00:24:20.759
and there's technologies called

00:24:20.759 --> 00:24:24.739
antisense oligonucleotides
or silencing RNAs,

00:24:24.739 --> 00:24:26.949
which can be used as therapeutics.

00:24:26.949 --> 00:24:29.299
And those are now
getting approved as well,

00:24:29.299 --> 00:24:31.769
so we're using RNA as therapeutics.

00:24:31.769 --> 00:24:34.119
These are very small strands of RNA.

00:24:34.119 --> 00:24:36.339
Now all of these areas
are opening up whole new,

00:24:36.339 --> 00:24:37.709
all of these technologies, I should say,

00:24:37.709 --> 00:24:40.829
they're opening up whole
new fields of medicine

00:24:40.829 --> 00:24:43.439
where we can treat diseases
that we haven't been able

00:24:43.439 --> 00:24:44.449
to treat in the past.

00:24:44.449 --> 00:24:46.829
When you think about
the rough numbers here,

00:24:46.829 --> 00:24:51.773
we can treat about 700 of
the roughly 8,000 to 10,000

00:24:51.773 --> 00:24:54.739
diseases that are in
the various compendium.

00:24:54.739 --> 00:24:56.609
So there's a lot of opportunity for us

00:24:56.609 --> 00:24:59.469
to improve human health,
and these technologies are

00:24:59.469 --> 00:25:01.239
helping us do that.

00:25:01.239 --> 00:25:03.839
The other big revolution
now is data science and AI,

00:25:03.839 --> 00:25:04.859
which I'm sure you've talked about.

00:25:04.859 --> 00:25:07.763
I know it's impacting education,
impacting our industry,

00:25:07.763 --> 00:25:08.639
as well.

00:25:08.639 --> 00:25:11.124
So how can you use AI to find new drugs,

00:25:11.124 --> 00:25:12.979
to develop drugs faster,

00:25:12.979 --> 00:25:15.204
to produce those drugs more efficiently?

00:25:15.204 --> 00:25:17.919
Probably many people would have seen

00:25:17.919 --> 00:25:22.519
that Google recently
published the structures,

00:25:22.519 --> 00:25:26.639
or DeepMind published the
structures of all proteins

00:25:26.639 --> 00:25:28.716
that they've been able
to identify thus far

00:25:28.716 --> 00:25:30.249
in the database.

00:25:30.249 --> 00:25:32.399
Which is again, a massive
accomplishment done

00:25:32.399 --> 00:25:33.999
through artificial intelligence.

00:25:34.956 --> 00:25:36.219
- You know, it is mind boggling.

00:25:36.219 --> 00:25:38.379
And I mean, I was thinking about asking,

00:25:38.379 --> 00:25:39.289
but when will we see this?

00:25:39.289 --> 00:25:40.749
But then I realized we
actually are seeing it.

00:25:40.749 --> 00:25:42.409
It's happening behind the scenes.

00:25:42.409 --> 00:25:44.129
I mean, even my wife,
who's a rheumatologist,

00:25:44.129 --> 00:25:46.381
she's saying, the medications, the drugs

00:25:46.381 --> 00:25:49.621
that she has access to even 20 years ago,

00:25:49.621 --> 00:25:52.079
they were just maintaining the pain,

00:25:52.079 --> 00:25:54.149
while now they can actually start to

00:25:54.149 --> 00:25:56.293
provide therapies and treat.

00:25:56.293 --> 00:26:00.299
If we go 10, 15 years in the
future, are there certain areas

00:26:00.299 --> 00:26:02.629
that you're particularly hopeful about?

00:26:02.629 --> 00:26:03.979
Or maybe you just want to jinx it yet?

00:26:03.979 --> 00:26:05.189
But you know, I'm sure there's areas

00:26:05.189 --> 00:26:06.999
that y'all are investing in that you think

00:26:06.999 --> 00:26:09.782
that there's space for some innovation.

00:26:11.079 --> 00:26:13.349
- Yeah, I think a few areas
that we have the opportunity,

00:26:13.349 --> 00:26:15.599
I mean, the goal of course is to get to

00:26:15.599 --> 00:26:17.759
a larger absolute efficacy gain.

00:26:17.759 --> 00:26:21.049
And can you really get to maybe
even cures and remissions?

00:26:21.049 --> 00:26:23.319
When you look at the whole
world of blood cancers,

00:26:23.319 --> 00:26:26.559
it's astonishing, I mean,
the progress we're making,

00:26:26.559 --> 00:26:29.069
and I'm hopeful that we can get to a world

00:26:29.069 --> 00:26:31.749
with all of the efforts in our sector

00:26:31.749 --> 00:26:34.879
and with academia and
government that maybe

00:26:34.879 --> 00:26:37.469
nobody needs to die from a blood cancer,

00:26:37.469 --> 00:26:39.559
given the pace of innovation
we're seeing there.

00:26:39.559 --> 00:26:41.327
That's pretty remarkable.

00:26:41.327 --> 00:26:43.979
When you look at certain rare diseases,

00:26:43.979 --> 00:26:46.349
we already have a medicine that when given

00:26:46.349 --> 00:26:50.219
to a child early enough,
can almost eliminate

00:26:50.219 --> 00:26:52.829
what would have otherwise have been

00:26:52.829 --> 00:26:55.679
a certainly terminal
disease for these children,

00:26:55.679 --> 00:26:59.219
and that's with a single
injection of that gene therapy.

00:26:59.219 --> 00:27:00.829
So I think in certain rare diseases,

00:27:00.829 --> 00:27:03.949
you're going to see astonishing progress.

00:27:03.949 --> 00:27:05.669
We're big believers in cardiovascular.

00:27:05.669 --> 00:27:06.969
It's still, it's worth remembering,

00:27:06.969 --> 00:27:08.759
the largest cause of death and disability

00:27:08.759 --> 00:27:11.529
in the world is heart disease,
even more than any infection.

00:27:11.529 --> 00:27:12.829
I haven't checked the numbers,

00:27:12.829 --> 00:27:16.039
it may still have out done
COVID last year as well

00:27:16.039 --> 00:27:18.549
in terms of overall death and disability.

00:27:18.549 --> 00:27:21.359
So I think advances in
cardiovascular disease are

00:27:21.359 --> 00:27:23.989
an opportunity to maybe
not have people die

00:27:23.989 --> 00:27:26.409
from heart attacks or
subsequent heart attacks.

00:27:26.409 --> 00:27:28.919
So those are areas of
tremendous opportunity,

00:27:28.919 --> 00:27:30.729
cardiovascular disease, cancer, et cetera.

00:27:30.729 --> 00:27:34.217
I mean, the places where we
have a lot of work to do is

00:27:34.217 --> 00:27:37.344
still understanding better
the biology of the brain,

00:27:37.344 --> 00:27:40.392
cognitive disorders,
psychiatric disorders,

00:27:40.392 --> 00:27:42.504
neurodegenerative disorders.

00:27:42.504 --> 00:27:45.396
The brain is a remarkable feat

00:27:45.396 --> 00:27:47.459
of billions of years of evolution,

00:27:47.459 --> 00:27:49.909
but still a lot of work
to do to understand

00:27:49.909 --> 00:27:52.472
what's going on in the
diseases of the brain.

00:27:53.839 --> 00:27:54.672
- Yeah, well, no,

00:27:54.672 --> 00:27:59.672
it makes very hopeful
for where we're going.

00:27:59.739 --> 00:28:02.259
And I know, I could talk
to you literally for hours,

00:28:02.259 --> 00:28:04.076
hopefully we'll get a chance to do that,

00:28:04.076 --> 00:28:05.539
but I just glanced at the clock.

00:28:05.539 --> 00:28:07.679
I was like, "Oh, we're
almost out of time."

00:28:07.679 --> 00:28:09.919
So, Vas, you know, I just
wanted to thank you incredibly.

00:28:09.919 --> 00:28:11.639
I have to say it, you know,

00:28:11.639 --> 00:28:13.509
and I'm not saying this
just because Novartis is

00:28:13.509 --> 00:28:15.129
one of the major
supporters of Kahn Academy.

00:28:15.129 --> 00:28:16.649
I think I would say this regardless,

00:28:16.649 --> 00:28:18.599
but talking to you is
incredibly refreshing,

00:28:18.599 --> 00:28:20.939
because there's a whole
CEO part of your job,

00:28:20.939 --> 00:28:23.269
which, you know, the CEO part of my job,

00:28:23.269 --> 00:28:25.945
which I try to do vaguely
competently is hard

00:28:25.945 --> 00:28:27.949
and we are about 200 folks.

00:28:27.949 --> 00:28:30.359
You were talking about a
hundred thousand plus employees.

00:28:30.359 --> 00:28:32.219
And so you must be incredible at that,

00:28:32.219 --> 00:28:33.979
but just speaking to you on science,

00:28:33.979 --> 00:28:35.469
the fact how deep you can go

00:28:35.469 --> 00:28:37.579
and that we can kind of nerd out together,

00:28:37.579 --> 00:28:41.179
while you're also managing
a hundred thousand plus,

00:28:41.179 --> 00:28:45.099
major pharmaceutical
entity that's giving drugs

00:28:45.099 --> 00:28:47.749
to 800 million people,
it's kind mind blowing,

00:28:47.749 --> 00:28:50.089
but at the same time heartening
to see how deep you can go.

00:28:50.089 --> 00:28:52.850
So thank you for your
willingness to talk to me.

00:28:52.850 --> 00:28:54.374
(Sal laughs)

00:28:54.374 --> 00:28:55.910
- I appreciate it Sal.

00:28:55.910 --> 00:28:57.069
I mean for anything, students out there.

00:28:57.069 --> 00:28:59.079
I'm still a scientist at heart.

00:28:59.079 --> 00:29:01.845
And I think, I hope I can prove
that you can be a scientist

00:29:01.845 --> 00:29:03.849
and work in the business world,

00:29:03.849 --> 00:29:05.879
but stay passionate about
science and medicine,

00:29:05.879 --> 00:29:07.537
because what wakes me up every day is

00:29:07.537 --> 00:29:09.542
those amazing findings that we have

00:29:09.542 --> 00:29:11.249
that can transform human lives.

00:29:11.249 --> 00:29:13.519
So always grateful to
be here and look forward

00:29:13.519 --> 00:29:15.599
to the next conversation, Sal.

00:29:15.599 --> 00:29:18.159
- Yeah, you know, amongst
like actors and stuff,

00:29:18.159 --> 00:29:20.059
there's the notion of a double
threat or of a triple threat.

00:29:20.059 --> 00:29:23.848
If you can act and sing
or act and sing and dance,

00:29:23.848 --> 00:29:26.836
you know how to manage and nerd out.

00:29:28.729 --> 00:29:30.559
Maybe you can actually sing and dance too.

00:29:30.559 --> 00:29:31.949
We'll, talk about that next time.

00:29:31.949 --> 00:29:32.782
- No, no.

00:29:32.782 --> 00:29:34.169
My kids would tell you
I cannot act or sing

00:29:34.169 --> 00:29:37.420
so they prefer me to
stick with being a CEO.

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:38.329
(both laugh)

00:29:38.329 --> 00:29:39.479
- Excellent, excellent.

00:29:39.479 --> 00:29:40.889
Well, thank you so much, Vas.

00:29:40.889 --> 00:29:44.309
And say hi to your kids, who
I know are Kahn Academy users

00:29:44.309 --> 00:29:46.429
and distributing Kahn Academy
stickers, which I appreciate.

00:29:46.429 --> 00:29:48.789
- All right, take care.

00:29:50.629 --> 00:29:52.139
- Well, thanks everyone for joining.

00:29:52.139 --> 00:29:53.936
I hope you enjoyed that
conversation with Vas.

00:29:53.936 --> 00:29:55.099
And I said it, I meant it,

00:29:55.099 --> 00:29:58.179
it's actually incredibly
refreshing when you meet someone

00:29:58.179 --> 00:30:00.200
who leads a multinational
organization like that,

00:30:00.200 --> 00:30:02.519
but who can also really go deep

00:30:02.519 --> 00:30:04.799
and clearly has a passion for what they do

00:30:04.799 --> 00:30:07.259
and the impact they're
having on the planet.

00:30:07.259 --> 00:30:09.077
So hopefully you found
that as inspiring as I did.

00:30:09.077 --> 00:30:12.149
And I look forward to
seeing you all next time.

00:30:12.149 --> 00:30:13.839
We are going to have a homeroom,

00:30:13.839 --> 00:30:16.319
it's going to be with our
chief learning officer,

00:30:16.319 --> 00:30:19.019
Kristen Dicerbo, gonna talk
to our head of content,

00:30:19.019 --> 00:30:21.869
Adam Green, about all of the new content

00:30:21.869 --> 00:30:23.526
and course refreshers on Khan Academy.

00:30:23.526 --> 00:30:25.019
And I don't want to pick favorites,

00:30:25.019 --> 00:30:26.549
because there's a lot of new content,

00:30:26.549 --> 00:30:28.688
but we've been doing a big science push.

00:30:28.688 --> 00:30:30.759
You know, that video I just talked about

00:30:30.759 --> 00:30:33.689
around gene expression
and around evolution,

00:30:33.689 --> 00:30:35.189
that's part of our science push.

00:30:35.189 --> 00:30:36.639
So, keep a look out for that,

00:30:36.639 --> 00:30:38.668
but you'll hear much more
about it on the Ed Talks

00:30:38.668 --> 00:30:41.399
with Kristen Dicerbo and Adam tomorrow.

00:30:41.399 --> 00:30:43.959
So with that, I will see y'all later.

00:30:43.959 --> 00:30:44.792
Onward.

